==============================================================================
PART A -- adjacency vs skew-Seidel IS the map x |-> 2x - 1
==============================================================================
  Read the tournament as a PROBABILITY matrix p_ij in {0, 1/2, 1}:
      p_ij = 1   if i beats j,   0   if j beats i,   1/2 on the diagonal (a tie).
  Then  S = 2p - 1  in {-1, 0, +1}  is exactly the skew-Seidel matrix of THM-1440.

  S = 2p-1 lands in {-1,0,1} and is skew:            True
  complement  p -> 1-p   IS   S -> -S:                True
  the tie p = 1/2 is the FIXED POINT, S = 0 (diagonal): True

  So: EVEN/ODD (multiplicative, S -> -S, the sign character) and
      POSITIVE/NEGATIVE (additive, p -> 1-p, a probability) are ONE involution
      in two coordinate systems, and 1/2 <-> 0 is its unique fixed point.

  Paley makes it literal: the skew-Seidel matrix of the Paley tournament is the
  LEGENDRE SYMBOL chi(j-i), whose value set is exactly {-1,0,+1}; its probability
  avatar (1+chi)/2 is the quadratic-residue indicator, with 1/2 sitting at 0.
    q= 7: value set of chi = [-1, 0, 1]   probability avatar (1+chi)/2 = [Fraction(0, 1), Fraction(1, 2), Fraction(1, 1)]
    q=11: value set of chi = [-1, 0, 1]   probability avatar (1+chi)/2 = [Fraction(0, 1), Fraction(1, 2), Fraction(1, 1)]
    q=19: value set of chi = [-1, 0, 1]   probability avatar (1+chi)/2 = [Fraction(0, 1), Fraction(1, 2), Fraction(1, 1)]

==============================================================================
PART B -- the FIBER FRACTION is the probability of an exact TIE
==============================================================================
  CLAUDE.md records  f(n) = (1/2)_{n-2}/(n-2)!,  GF = (1-x)^{-1/2}.
  Three expressions, all equal:
      (1/2)_k / k!        the Pochhammer form (the repo's)
      C(2k,k) / 4^k       the CENTRAL BINOMIAL PROBABILITY = P(exact tie in 2k
                          fair coin flips)
      E[U^k] / k!         with U = T^2/2, T ~ N(0,1)   (THM-1500's d=1 moment)

  k     (1/2)_k/k!    C(2k,k)/4^k      E[U^k]/k!   equal    decimal
  0              1              1              1    True   1.000000
  1            1/2            1/2            1/2    True   0.500000
  2            3/8            3/8            3/8    True   0.375000
  3           5/16           5/16           5/16    True   0.312500
  4         35/128         35/128         35/128    True   0.273438
  5         63/256         63/256         63/256    True   0.246094
  6       231/1024       231/1024       231/1024    True   0.225586
  7       429/2048       429/2048       429/2048    True   0.209473
  8     6435/32768     6435/32768     6435/32768    True   0.196381
  all three agree: True

  So the repo's fiber fraction IS a tie probability -- the 1/2 is a FAIR COIN, and
  the 'two-sheeted branched cover' (1-x)^{-1/2} is its square root.  The tie is the
  0 of {-1,0,1} and the 1/2 of {0,1/2,1}: the SAME point in the two coordinates.

==============================================================================
PART C -- THM-1500's admissible ladder IS the three-element set
==============================================================================
  THM-1500: U = chi^2_d / 2 has MGF Phi(x) = (1-x)^{-d/2}; the master equation
  Phi(-s g(s)) = 1/(1+s) forces 1 + s g(s) = (1+s)^{2/d}, POLYNOMIAL iff 2/d is a
  positive integer, i.e. d in {1,2}.  d = 0 is the obstructed case (n=2).

  d  exponent d/2    2x-1  n = 2+d       g(s)                     status
  0             0      -1        2 log(1+s)/s   OBSTRUCTED (GMC(2) open)
  1           1/2       0        3      2 + s     MINIMAL counterexample
  2             1       1        4          1         the 4-term example

  The exponent ladder {0, 1/2, 1} maps under x |-> 2x-1 onto {-1, 0, +1}.
  And the MINIMAL counterexample n = 3 sits at the FIXED POINT (1/2 <-> 0) --
  the same 1/2 as the fiber fraction, and the same 1/2 that makes (1+s)^{2/d}
  a PERFECT SQUARE.  Square root and square are the same one-half.

==============================================================================
PART D -- the telescoping principle, read in the two coordinate systems
==============================================================================
  THM-1520/1540: the nullcone is the STRICTLY ONE-SIDED charge support, and
  failure REQUIRES two-sided support.  In the two coordinates:

    SIGN view {-1,0,+1}:  which signs the charge support hits.
        {+1} only  -> nullcone.   {-1} only -> nullcone.   both -> the open case.
    BALANCE view {0,1/2,1}:  b = (fraction of the support with positive charge).
        b = 1 -> nullcone.   b = 0 -> nullcone.   0 < b < 1 -> the open case.

  So THE NULLCONE IS THE ENDPOINTS AND THE HARD CASE IS THE INTERIOR, with the
  balanced point b = 1/2 the deepest interior point.  That is the same statement
  as PART A's involution having a unique fixed point, one level up.

                 P     charge signs   balance b   in nullcone?
                 Z              [1]           1           True
            Z^2-3Z              [1]           1           True
            W+5W^2             [-1]           0           True
               Z+W          [-1, 1]         1/2          False
             Z^2+W          [-1, 1]         1/2          False
              ZW+Z           [0, 1]         1/2          False

